Task: post-merge follow-up (track aguiproto_20260908, after completion)

A peer session reported ag-ui PR 2678 merged and made three claims. Each
was checked against the SDK rather than taken on trust.

1. 'Drop the fork, pin v0.0.0-20260908160240-409ad608f702.' Already done
   in 42a3398, same version. No action.

2. 'RunFinishedOutcome can emit two shapes the TypeScript client rejects;
   never pass an empty interrupt slice.' Verified not reachable here:
   WithInterruptOutcome is called only from finishWithInterrupts, which
   ProcessEvent invokes solely under len(pendingInterrupts) > 0. The
   success path uses WithSuccessOutcome() and threads.go emits no outcome
   at all. All three shapes safe, no change needed.

3. 'Put ADK token usage under your OWN keys, not ag-ui.' Correct, and
   acted on here.

On (3), the peer's advice contradicted spec FR3, which explicitly asked
for metadata['ag-ui'].tokenUsage 'matching the first-party SDKs'
token-usage shape'. Checked the claim rather than either side's word:
- types.AGUIMetadataKey's own doc says it is 'reserved for AG-UI's own
  use' and 'every other key is user space'.
- No tokenUsage shape exists anywhere in the Go SDK. The hits are in
  metadata_test.go, which uses the name at the top level AND under
  ag-ui in different fixtures — merge-semantics tests, not a contract.

So FR3's justification was unverifiable and its placement put launcher
data in reserved space. Deviating from the spec was the user's call, not
mine to make silently; raised it with the evidence and they chose to move.

Changes:
- agui/internal/stream/metadata.go: aguiMetadataKey removed; tokenUsage
  folded into the adk block; eventMetadata simplified to one namespace.
- agui/event_metadata_test.go: usage asserted under adk, plus a new test
  that nothing is written under types.AGUIMetadataKey at all.
- agui/doc.go: records why the reserved key is left alone.

Goldens unaffected: none of the three baseline runs set UsageMetadata.

Verification: eventMetadata and tokenUsage both 100%; gofmt -l silent,
go vet ./... silent, CI=true go test ./... -count=1 all packages ok.
